WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.730
Andy Warhol was born three times: the first
to his parents, as Andy Warhola.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.090
The second time as a commercial artist, Raggedy
Andy.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:36.860
After a failed assassination, he was reborn
the final time, as the artistic paragon and

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.790
cultural revolutionary remembered worldwide
as Andy Warhol.

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:45.790
Child number four to a poor immigrant family,
raised in a tenement building during the Great

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:51.510
Depression, the story of Andy is the story
of the American dream itself- with a little

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:58.629
extra obsession, drugs, and murder sprinkled
on top, perfect for his Hollywood heart.

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:04.739
Bringing Modern Art to the Modern Age

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:09.899
It is impossible to overstate his contributions,
not just to painting, but to the entire science

00:01:09.899 --> 00:01:10.930
of seeing.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.860
The idea that a mass-market object could be
elevated to the status of art just by how

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:19.960
it's looked at is such a profound rejection
of every standard which came before it that

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:25.820
we no longer even recognize it as revolutionary,
it has been internalized whole-cloth as The

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.330
New Truth: The audience decides what is art..

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:35.190
Just as much as democracy is a rejection of
the divine right of kings, Andy's contributions

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:40.380
to the American perspective are so ingrained
that we no longer recognize them as his.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.660
Art is attention.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:45.520
“In the future we’ll all be famous for
15 minutes,” Warhol said that.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.300
The term "superstar" is one he coined, to
describe the hippies and drag queens he used

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:51.520
for camera fodder.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.259
Andy understood better than any of his contemporaries
that what the public wants is to be told that

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:02.610
they matter, that the trappings of consumer
culture are part of a grander narrative.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:08.069
Even in television and movies, every behind
the scenes feature pits heroic creatives against

00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:10.390
the evil studio money-men.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:15.400
It's not enough to enjoy a piece of entertainment,
the work itself must be the product of this

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.290
titanic struggle or it doesn't have any weight
to it.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:24.670
The drama gives it significance, no one would
remember Firefly if it wasn't defined by the

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:29.849
conflict between Joss Wheadon and a bunch
of nameless executives who loved blowing huge

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.060
opportunities.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.560
That's the drama which inspired Andy to paint
Liza Minelli as her career and marriage began

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:37.560
to crumble.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.200
That's why he painted his Death &amp; Disaster
series, one of which holds the record for

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.590
Warhol's most expensive work [Show Silver
Car Crash on screen].

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:49.290
America loves an underdog, and there is no
bigger underdog than the victim in a fight

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:50.350
against yourself.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:55.990
It's our hunger for celebrities which drives
their self-destruction, and here Andy comes

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:01.570
along, turning a silk screened headshot into
the kind of icon they build chapels around,

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:06.310
equal parts relic and lecture on the cyclical
hazard of icon worship.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:11.990
The same hazard which would later temporarily
cost him his life.

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:16.400
A Pale Boy from Pittsburg

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.069
Andy grew up in the shadow of the St. John
Chrysostom Byzantine Catholic Church, which

00:03:21.069 --> 00:03:24.540
is known for its huge and ornate wall of gold-leafed
icons.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.930
[Show icons] With their unreadably beatific
expressions and similar poses, this became

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:32.080
the genesis for Andy's experimentation with
repeated images.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.630
The worshipful quality of his portraits, how
the use of color elevates the mortal to the

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.390
eternal, everything is here.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:42.030
You can trace every single one of his artistic
motifs to this one place.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:46.460
There's a direct line between spending eight
hours a week in church, staring at a gold-leafed

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:52.140
image of Mary and then thirty years later,
painting Marilyn Monroe on top of more gold

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:53.140
leaf.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.459
The conflation of consumption and worship
is rampant in Warhol's work, because that

00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:00.880
is the language America uses to express desire
and reverence.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.900
They eat what they worship, celebrity is Communion.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.040
Success in America is being incorporated into
the greater whole, like how the calcium in

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.900
milk becomes bone and teeth.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.930
Andy is the calcium of modernity, that’s
why is hair was white..

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:18.810
In the third grade, he had to take a temporary
leave of absence when a nerve disease named

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:22.990
St. Vitus' Dance plagued him with involuntary
muscle spasms.

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.840
He would struggle to write his name on the
chalkboard and other children would bully

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.940
him so much that he simply refused to return.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.280
It's this period of affliction and sickness
in which the seeds of Warhol's genius sprout.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.900
To keep him occupied, his mother provided
him with Hollywood magazines, newsletters

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:42.060
from fan groups, and plenty of paper for experimentation,
going so far as to build a darkroom in the

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:43.710
basement for his photography.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:48.120
Warhol began collecting autographed pictures,
with his Shirley Temple as the jewel of the

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:49.120
set.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:52.440
To incentivize creation, his mother would
provide a piece of chocolate for every completed

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.470
work of art.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:57.820
This motivation clearly stuck with Andy, because
after he recovered, he continued taking art

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.960
classes through elementary and high school,
pursuing a college degree at Carnegie Mellon

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.639
in commercial art.

00:05:03.639 --> 00:05:09.310
Success is a Job in New York

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:13.150
After graduating in 1949, Andy Warhola moved
to New York City.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.610
He was always withdrawn, even before making
it a central part of his public persona.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:22.350
A shy boy with a large nose and an inconsistent
complexion, which he would later attempt to

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:23.780
address through surgery.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:26.430
He was insecure about everything but his talents.

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:30.830
He quickly attracted respect from the art
directors at magazines for his technical skills,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:34.970
and the unique appeal of his self-developed
"blotted line technique," a style of simple

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:38.820
printmaking using a wet ink drawing pressed
against transfer paper.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:44.150
Perhaps more so even than his art, Andy developed
a reputation for being, well, weird.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:48.190
In addition to his physical uniqueness, his
clothes were tattered, his shoes broken-in,

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:53.460
and his art was delivered in crumpled paper
bags, rather than professional portfolios.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:56.670
In a phrase, he stuck out.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:00.480
Glamour Magazine was the first to commission
him to illustrate shoes for an article titled

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.840
"What is Success?"

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.870
Perhaps accidentally, he was credited as Warhol,
and his rebirth as a commercial artist was

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:07.870
complete.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:12.050
His whimsical style soon won him contracts
with Vogue, Columbia Records, and Tiffany

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.050
&amp; Co.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:17.060
His upwards trajectory only increased when,
in 1952, his mother arrived on the stoop of

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:20.590
his apartment to inform him that she had sold
the family house.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:24.840
Almost immediately, they start collaborating
on commercial works as well as art books for

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:25.840
friends.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.450
She designed and hand-wrote a record label
for New York busker icon Moondog which even

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:31.550
won an award.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.919
No matter how much Andy may have worried about
his mother embarrassing him in front of his

00:06:34.919 --> 00:06:40.230
trendy friends, they lived and worked together
for the next 20 years, until her death.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:44.590
As hardworking as her son was, her unique
eye and flowing script helped refine the style

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:47.320
that brought him so much praise and success.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.419
However, this wasn't enough for the ambitious
young artist.

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:55.600
More than security, he wanted to be respected
for his talents, and even admired.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:59.780
He wanted the world to know why he mattered.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:05.169
Boys &amp; Other Headaches

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:09.169
It's around this flourishing point in his
career that Andy comes to grips with his same-sex

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:12.690
attraction, even if fulfilling his ambitions
proved difficult.

00:07:12.690 --> 00:07:16.990
His first kiss didn't come until 25, and he
wasn't able to perform the rest of that night's

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:18.020
duties.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.100
After being rejected during a trip to Honolulu,
he complained to a friend that he had "gone

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:26.050
around the world with a boy and not even received
one kiss."

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:30.259
Most biographers are content to say that he
was gay in an era where that could get you

00:07:30.259 --> 00:07:33.949
arrested, but the truth is somewhat more convoluted.

00:07:33.949 --> 00:07:38.730
The difficulty Warhol had with relationships
and the consummation thereof, as well as his

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:43.990
habit of incorporating voyeurism and celibacy
into his creative process, has inspired much

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:46.889
academic speculation as to his possible asexuality.

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:52.740
He himself claimed to be a virgin in 1980,
though this is contradicted by testimony from

00:07:52.740 --> 00:08:00.070
former lovers and that time he sought treatment
for an STD in 1960.

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:05.900
It's an extremely Andy Warhol move to saturate
your life and work with sex, only to deny

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:07.270
its ultimate power.

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:12.070
Negating sex in an era where it had never
been more ubiquitous was just another way

00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:17.870
in which he stood out from the crowd, an observer
of the culture chasing his every move.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:23.910
15 Rejections Based on the Friendship of Truman
Capote

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:27.960
For those of you unfamiliar with New York's
publishing scene in the 1950s, Truman Capote

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.889
was a young literary hotshot who was in the
process of blowing everyone's mind by somehow

00:08:32.889 --> 00:08:38.639
managing to succeed in writing strictly on
merit alone, instead of a college alumni network

00:08:38.639 --> 00:08:40.560
and someone elses’ money.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.640
He was stylishly gay, beloved by the art world,
and Andy Warhol wanted to be him.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:49.160
He started leaving little illustrations in
Capote's mailbox.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:53.199
It eventually escalated to Warhol waiting
outside his door, hoping to develop a friendship

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:54.529
through sheer insistence.

00:08:54.529 --> 00:08:56.089
It didn't work.

00:08:56.089 --> 00:08:59.769
Capote rejected him, later describing him
as "one of those hopeless people you know

00:08:59.769 --> 00:09:04.119
nothing ever's going to happen to" and "a
hopeless, born loser."

00:09:04.119 --> 00:09:08.369
A rejection like that can sure make a fellow
appreciate being ghosted.

00:09:08.369 --> 00:09:13.509
Undaunted, Warhol turned this rejection into
fuel for his first fine art show, 15 Drawings

00:09:13.509 --> 00:09:15.529
Based on the Works of Truman Capote.

00:09:15.529 --> 00:09:20.230
Despite being masterful works of flowing,
unbroken lines, none sold.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.760
This set the pattern for his early forays
into fine art, on the rare occasions he managed

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.079
to earn gallery space.

00:09:27.079 --> 00:09:31.950
In the sharpest possible contrast to his continued
success as a commercial illustrator, gallery

00:09:31.950 --> 00:09:34.930
owners and art dealers refused to help him
sell his work.

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:39.069
Before he dismantled it utterly, there was
a fairly substantial barrier between commercial

00:09:39.069 --> 00:09:40.069
and fine artists, [said while maintaining
eye contact] because people lucky enough to

00:09:40.069 --> 00:09:41.069
succeed in fine arts don't need to work for
a living.

00:09:41.069 --> 00:09:42.069
[Intentional, awkward pause.]

00:09:42.069 --> 00:09:43.069
Thankfully, that's no longer the case today.

00:09:43.069 --> 00:09:44.069
[Even longer, even more intentional awkward
pause.]

00:09:44.069 --> 00:09:45.069
But even moreso than that, Andy was gay and
off-putting in a city that eats weakness.

00:09:45.069 --> 00:09:49.529
Granted, "too gay for the New York art scene"
sounds like the setup for a joke where the

00:09:49.529 --> 00:09:55.309
punchline is Louis CK masturbating, but this
was absolutely the case in the 50s.

00:09:55.309 --> 00:10:01.209
Then, the city was ruled by Jackson Pollock
and his band of whiskey-soaked heterosexual

00:10:01.209 --> 00:10:02.439
abstract impressionists.

00:10:02.439 --> 00:10:07.110
That's what art dealers and buyers wanted,
when Warhol showed up with his second collection

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:14.279
of ink drawings, Studies for a Boy Book, there
was simply no market for his kind of obsession.

00:10:14.279 --> 00:10:16.019
It scared people.

00:10:16.019 --> 00:10:20.329
But no matter how good his work was, everywhere
Warhol went, he was rejected for being too

00:10:20.329 --> 00:11:17.509
swish, too camp, too weird, those same traits
which would soon bring him more fame and admirers

00:11:17.509 --> 00:11:25.059
than he could handle.

00:11:25.059 --> 00:11:53.980
Pop and Circumstance

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:57.759
While Warhol is the most widely known Pop
artist, he hardly invented the genre.

00:11:57.759 --> 00:12:02.519
He was inspired most directly by the work
of James Rosenquist and Roy Lichtenstein,

00:12:02.519 --> 00:12:08.069
who both rose to prominence painting flat
representations of advertisements and other

00:12:08.069 --> 00:12:09.870
cultural detritus.

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:14.329
Warhol set to work immediately, painting scenes
taken from Nancy cartoons and comic book wig

00:12:14.329 --> 00:12:15.329
advertisements.

00:12:15.329 --> 00:12:17.689
The work was not popular, even with Andy.

00:12:17.689 --> 00:12:21.610
There's a period of approximately fourteen
months between Warhol's decision to pursue

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:24.660
fine art, and his first major success.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:28.879
In 1961, he painted two versions of a Coca-Cola
bottle, one full of the brush strokes and

00:12:28.879 --> 00:12:31.790
human marks of creation, and the other without.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:35.679
Coca-Cola [2] was as sterile and hard-lined
as a neon sign.

00:12:35.679 --> 00:12:38.939
No trace of the artist remained, nothing human.

00:12:38.939 --> 00:12:41.529
In short, it blew the first one out of the
water.

00:12:41.529 --> 00:12:44.170
His friends called it naked, savage, barren.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:47.959
Nothing of the painting existed between the
message and the audience, no gradients or

00:12:47.959 --> 00:12:49.360
shadows for the eye to hide in.

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:50.360
He had found his style.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.149
Soup, There It Is!

00:12:53.149 --> 00:13:02.329
Murial Latow is the name of the woman who
inspired Andy Warhol to paint 32 cans of Campbell's

00:13:02.329 --> 00:13:03.329
soup.

00:13:03.329 --> 00:13:07.209
When he asked her for ideas, she said simply,
"you like money, you should paint pictures

00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:08.209
of money.

00:13:08.209 --> 00:13:13.529
you should paint something everyone sees every
day, that everyone recognizes, like a can

00:13:13.529 --> 00:13:14.529
of soup."

00:13:14.529 --> 00:13:18.070
For Andy, Campbell's soup was more than just
the kind of convenient meal which didn't interrupt

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:21.139
his workflow, it held a deep personal significance.

00:13:21.139 --> 00:13:25.319
During his childhood, his mother suffered
from colon cancer, and had to be hospitalized

00:13:25.319 --> 00:13:26.319
for six weeks.

00:13:26.319 --> 00:13:31.170
Everyday she was gone, his older brother would
make him a soup and sandwich, and reassure

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:33.069
him that his mother would be okay.

00:13:33.069 --> 00:13:37.920
That's what these cans represent, that's what
they're inspired by, a sobbing child desperate

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.329
to believe that his mother will be okay, taking
their daily ritual of soup.

00:13:42.329 --> 00:13:46.970
Consumption becomes reverence, and through
his tithe, his mother was restored to him.

00:13:46.970 --> 00:13:51.250
I was not exaggerating when I said that Andy
painted religious icons, I think to him, the

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:53.879
soup can was a symbol of resurrection.

00:13:53.879 --> 00:13:58.399
It certainly resurrected his career, Warhol
painted one portrait of each available variety

00:13:58.399 --> 00:14:02.399
of Campbell's soup, and when they displayed
in a Hollywood gallery, he brought the Pop

00:14:02.399 --> 00:14:04.549
Art movement to the west coast.

00:14:04.549 --> 00:14:08.660
Instead of waiting for the world of fine art
to accept him, he battered down the doors

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:09.779
instead.

00:14:09.779 --> 00:14:14.279
Taming the Silver Silkscreen

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:20.509
August 1962 marks another revolution in his
career, the incorporation of drawn and photographed

00:14:20.509 --> 00:14:22.519
silkscreens in his paintings.

00:14:22.519 --> 00:14:26.970
The inconsistencies of his blotted lines are
replaced, almost overnight, with the divine

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:31.139
accidents of an artistic method reduced to
assembly line efficiency.

00:14:31.139 --> 00:14:35.980
He focuses first on Hollywood icons from his
youth, followed by stars in decline, like

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:37.019
Liza Minnelli.

00:14:37.019 --> 00:14:41.179
Andy got started on his series of Marilyn
Monroe paintings the same day she died, and

00:14:41.179 --> 00:14:44.559
eventually debuted them to huge public response.

00:14:44.559 --> 00:14:48.970
Using a still from the 1953 movie Niagara,
Andy took an image of her in the prime of

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:54.079
her youth and joy, and through countless repeated
silk screenings, eventually slowly transformed

00:14:54.079 --> 00:15:00.519
the joyful smile into a bitter grimace, the
kind of rictus grin which precedes oblivion.

00:15:00.519 --> 00:15:05.359
No piece makes this more clear than the Marilyn
Diptych, a sequence of colored portraits juxtaposed

00:15:05.359 --> 00:15:10.070
next to a row of washed out and incomplete
black and white images, the commentary is

00:15:10.070 --> 00:15:11.070
obvious.

00:15:11.070 --> 00:15:14.819
Not only does it resemble a film strip, but
the increasingly vague monochrome transfers

00:15:14.819 --> 00:15:18.480
really capture how it feels to wear out the
memory of a loved one.

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:23.380
It's more than just death, it's the very public
death that only the extremely famous get to

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:27.430
live through, the slow relegation to the b-roll
of history.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:31.509
Perhaps not coincidentally, this is also the
era he starts producing and directing his

00:15:31.509 --> 00:15:32.509
own films.

00:15:32.509 --> 00:15:37.379
Eight hours of the Empire State Building vanishing
into the night sky, six hours of his boyfriend

00:15:37.379 --> 00:15:41.709
sleeping, the kind of deliberately impenetrable
stuff that gets modern-day freshmen kicked

00:15:41.709 --> 00:15:42.799
out of film school.

00:15:42.799 --> 00:15:48.069
He also produced the campy and unlicensed
Batman Dracula, which is exactly what you

00:15:48.069 --> 00:15:49.069
imagine.

00:15:49.069 --> 00:15:52.620
Just two years later, Adam West's knowingly
tongue-in-cheek take on the caped crusader

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:53.989
debuts with the POW!

00:15:53.989 --> 00:15:54.989
BAP!

00:15:54.989 --> 00:15:56.970
Batman TV and movie shows your parents remember.

00:15:56.970 --> 00:16:00.819
Though it was only ever displayed in galleries
or as part of shows, these screenings were

00:16:00.819 --> 00:16:06.089
taking place in New York City and Hollywood
at the height of Warhol's fame, scenes a popular

00:16:06.089 --> 00:16:08.819
actor like Adam West would absolutely have
access to.

00:16:08.819 --> 00:16:13.459
The influence is undeniable, and remains a
core part of Batman to this day.

00:16:13.459 --> 00:16:17.399
Even Superheroes were not beyond the reach
of Warhol.

00:16:17.399 --> 00:16:25.059
Can't Spell "Factory" Without U!

00:16:25.059 --> 00:16:30.189
In 1963, Warhol hired a friend to decorate
his new work and party space with mirrors

00:16:30.189 --> 00:16:32.629
and tinfoil, and the Silver Factory was born.

00:16:32.629 --> 00:16:36.929
To Warhol, Silver represented both the coming
space age future, and the silver screens of

00:16:36.929 --> 00:16:38.769
Hollywood's glamorous past.

00:16:38.769 --> 00:16:41.490
It also represented a mirror and narcissism.

00:16:41.490 --> 00:16:46.199
In short, it reflects now, it gives us what
we want: Us.

00:16:46.199 --> 00:16:50.529
His entourage immediately starts using it
as the base of operations for a nearly continuous

00:16:50.529 --> 00:16:55.079
stream of artistic creation and revelry which
would continue for most of the rest of the

00:16:55.079 --> 00:16:56.079
decade.

00:16:56.079 --> 00:16:59.799
It became the de-facto center of the New York
art scene, Bob Dylan would go there, Mick

00:16:59.799 --> 00:17:03.959
Jagger, Salvadore Dali, Liza Minelli, William
S. Burroughs, even David Bowie called themselves

00:17:03.959 --> 00:17:04.959
regulars.

00:17:04.959 --> 00:17:06.110
In one room, people were doing speed.

00:17:06.110 --> 00:17:09.390
In another, people were filming a music video
for The Velvet Underground.

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:13.350
Warhol would be making silk screens in one
corner of the workshop while at the other,

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:14.670
drag queens vamped.

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:16.689
It was, in every sense, bacchanalian.

00:17:16.689 --> 00:17:21.160
And just like in the stories of the Bacchantes,
Warhol would find himself torn asunder by

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:24.000
the very party he cultivated.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:29.460
Never Trust a Playwright

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:33.450
The central irony in the assassination of
Andy Warhol is that he wasn't even the initial

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:36.930
target, he was the understudy for his own
death.

00:17:36.930 --> 00:17:41.620
Valerie Solanis stuffed the guns in her coat
that day, intending to murder her publisher,

00:17:41.620 --> 00:17:42.920
Maurice Girodas.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:47.930
She waited outside his apartment for three
hours in an unseasonably warm hat and overcoat,

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:51.610
but lucky for him, he missed that particular
appointment with Fate.

00:17:51.610 --> 00:17:56.150
Crestfallen but unwilling to abandon her plans
for the day, she headed next to Warhol's Factory.

00:17:56.150 --> 00:18:01.240
He knew Valerie as the author of an extremely
graphic anti-male play so violent that he

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:03.930
initially suspected her to be a police informant.

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:08.510
She believed him to be part of a conspiracy
to steal this script, when instead it had

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:09.600
been misplaced.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:14.490
When they arrived in his office, she drew
her two automatic pistols, and wounded Warhol.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:18.950
She was preparing to execute an assistant
when the elevator opened, and she simply fled,

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:21.660
turning herself over to police custody several
hours later.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:25.540
She left behind a paper bag at the factory,
containing her address, a backup gun, and

00:18:25.540 --> 00:18:27.200
a single sanitary napkin.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:31.520
The idea that the assassination of Andy Warhol
was intended as some kind of theatrical performance

00:18:31.520 --> 00:18:36.860
or social critique has been discussed quite
seriously in academic circles, Valerie's work

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:42.700
is obscene and confrontational, including
the much-maligned SCUM Manifesto and the one-woman

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:46.450
organization it spawned, the Society for Cutting
Up Men.

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:51.070
Shortly thereafter, many other artists, including
Chris Burden and his museum-destroying sculpture

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:54.090
Samson, would incorporate gunplay into their
work.

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:58.490
For a certain type of New Yorker, the temporary
death of Warhol marked the end of the innocent

00:18:58.490 --> 00:18:59.490
60s.

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:02.990
The ramifications of the shooting would linger
with Warhol for the rest of his days, which

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:08.500
is understandable considering he only survived
because the doctor reached into his chest

00:19:08.500 --> 00:19:09.680
and hand-massaged his heart.

00:19:09.680 --> 00:19:13.550
No one jumps back from that without a scar.

00:19:13.550 --> 00:19:18.100
He was required to wear a surgical girdle
around the operating site, much like the silver

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:20.820
screen darlings he so admired in his childhood.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:24.930
Far from being shocked out of his semi-affected
malaise, Warhol seemed to retreat into the

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:30.690
unreality of it all, remarking ""Before I
was shot, I always thought that I was more

00:19:30.690 --> 00:19:35.270
half-there than all-there—I always suspected
that I was watching TV instead of living life.

00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:39.370
People sometimes say that the way things happen
in movies is unreal, but actually it's the

00:19:39.370 --> 00:19:42.260
way things happen in life that's unreal.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:46.540
The movies make emotions look so strong and
real, whereas when things really do happen

00:19:46.540 --> 00:19:49.770
to you, it's like watching television—you
don't feel anything.

00:19:49.770 --> 00:19:53.540
Right when I was being shot and ever since,
I knew that I was watching television.

00:19:53.540 --> 00:19:56.450
The channels switch, but it's all television."

00:19:56.450 --> 00:20:00.240
While this brought down the gate on the open-door
factory, Warhol's reputation as a nightclub

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.020
regular was just getting started.

00:20:02.020 --> 00:20:05.650
He would spend much of the 70s hobnobbing
with New York royalty in the famed Studio

00:20:05.650 --> 00:20:10.481
54, taking it all in as fuel for high-paying
commissions of celebrities, socialites, and

00:20:10.481 --> 00:20:11.950
an actual Shah.

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:16.010
He founded Interview, a celebrity interview
magazine which is still being published.

00:20:16.010 --> 00:20:18.170
For a normal person, that's a huge amount
of work.

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:22.060
Most people never even get to paint a vizier,
let alone a full Shah, but for the creator

00:20:22.060 --> 00:20:28.560
of the Exploding Plastic Inevitable, the orgy-cum-art
show which inspired "The Rave," it must have

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.270
felt like a living retirement.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:33.570
Rather than spending the final decade of his
life resting on the laurels of the man who

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:37.880
invented Today, Andy started collaborating
with many young talents in the underground

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:38.880
arts scene, including Macklemore's personal
favorite, Basquiat.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:39.880
For those of you who don't recognize the name,
approximately two hundred years ago, Macklemore

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:40.880
wrote a song about thrift shops.

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:41.880
A shop is like a real life Amazon where they
only carry one type of thing, and it's never

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:42.880
what you need.

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:43.880
But I digress, Basquiat was a controversial
figure in the art world for his propensity

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:44.880
of cranking out a huge number of visually
arresting but thematically opaque works which

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:45.880
he would sell for huge amounts of money.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:46.880
How soon the student overtakes the master!

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:47.880
His final gallery showing would debut across
the street from the nunnery which houses Leonardo

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:48.880
da Vinci's Last Supper.

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:49.880
Fittingly enough, The Last Supper was the
subject matter, something Warhol must have

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:51.810
delighted in, because he returned to traditional
brush painting for the first time in twenty

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:52.810
years.

00:20:52.810 --> 00:20:57.270
He and Basquiat produced well over a hundred
works, including Ten Punching Bags, which

00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:00.430
features the face of Jesus painted across,
you guessed it, ten punching bags.

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:05.130
Warhol still holds the record for most works
displayed on a single subject.

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:09.090
In February of 1987, he died while recovering
from what should have been routine gallbladder

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:10.090
surgery.

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:14.070
The public wake shut down large portions of
New York and Pittsburgh, where he was buried

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:15.180
next to his parents.

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:16.530
He was 58.

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:21.340
For someone who had lived through so much,
the depression, World War 2, an assassination,

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:25.420
dying due to a moment's accidental neglect
feels anticlimactic.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:41.330
Unfinished, like a silk screen with too little
paint.

